<TopicPage slug="subjects/urban-planning/foundational/planning-history-and-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Planning is the <strong>deliberate attempt to shape how cities grow</strong> — and its history
    is a century-long argument about <em>who</em> gets to do the shaping. The field's first heroes
    drew total visions: Haussmann's boulevards, Howard's garden cities, Burnham's Chicago. The
    mid-twentieth century bureaucratised that impulse into the <em>rational model</em> — data,
    alternatives, evaluation — enforced through zoning. Then came the backlash: Jacobs showed that
    master plans were destroying exactly the urban vitality they claimed to serve, Arnstein showed
    that "participation" was often theatre, and a series of theorists rebuilt the field around
    communication and justice instead of blueprints.
  </p>
  <p>
    Understanding planning theory is not academic decoration. Every zoning hearing is a live
    collision between the rational model (traffic studies), communicative practice (public
    consultation) and the just city (who benefits, who is displaced). Planners need all three
    vocabularies at once.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three theories, in one ascent: rational → communicative → just" widget="equationStep" config={ptCfg} />

  <h2>The evolution of cities and planning</h2>
  <p>
    Planning as a profession crystallised between 1890 and 1930, in three overlapping movements.
    <strong>City beautiful</strong> treated monumental design as civic reform. <strong>Garden
    cities</strong> — Howard's idea, built at Letchworth (1903) and Welwyn — invented the planned,
    green-belted settlement and, eventually, the new-town movement. <strong>Zoning</strong>, first
    comprehensively in New York (1916) and constitutionalised in the US by <em>Euclid v. Ambler</em>
    (1926), gave planning its everyday legal machinery. The modernist Athens Charter (1933)
    consolidated a functionalist orthodoxy — zones for living, working, circulation — that
    dominated rebuilding after 1945 and that Jacobs, then a generation of critics, dismantled
    from the sidewalk up.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="key moments in planning thought, 1853–2010" widget="timeline" config={upTl} />

  <h2>Planning ethics</h2>
  <p>
    Because planning decisions redistribute value — a rezoning can move millions of dollars from
    one property owner to another — ethics is not optional garnish. The professional codes (AICP in
    the US, RTPI's in the UK) converge on a few durable principles: <strong>serve the public
    interest</strong> while acknowledging it is plural and contested; <strong>give affected people
    a genuine voice</strong>, not a performance of consultation; <strong>protect the interests of
    those least able to defend them</strong>; <strong>disclose conflicts of interest</strong>; and
    <strong>be truthful with data</strong> — a planner's forecasts are advocacy with decimal
    points. Arnstein's 1969 ladder remains the sharpest test: is the public being informed,
    consulted, partnering — or merely placated?
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hall &amp; Tewdwr-Jones, Urban and Regional Planning; Fainstein &amp; DeFilippis, Readings in Planning Theory; Jacobs, The Death and Life of Great American Cities (1961); Arnstein, "A Ladder of Citizen Participation" (1969); Fainstein, The Just City (2010); AICP Code of Ethics.</p>
